---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro'
import StatTile from '../components/StatTile.astro'
import { assertion } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const life = assertion('lima-tracts-live-five-years-less')
const gap = assertion('the-health-gap-is-a-gap-in-conditions')
const dying = assertion('the-county-dies-younger-than-its-state')
const overdose = assertion('overdose-deaths-fell-by-two-thirds')

// The county's rate at every third window, from the assertion's own figures, against the national
// rate for the same nine windows. The national series is typed out because it is the control and
// not the claim; both are in the corpus node the assertion cites.
const nationalYpll = [7705, 7535, 7345, 7090, 6704, 6601, 6901, 7282, 8352]
const ypllSpec = line(dying.figures.map((f) => f.label), [
    { name: 'Allen County', y: dying.figures.map((f) => f.value) },
    { name: 'United States', y: nationalYpll },
  ], {
  yTitle: 'Years lost before 75, per 100,000',
})

const overdoseSpec = bar(
  overdose.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  overdose.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Fall from peak', tickSuffix: '%', labelValues: true },
)

// The eleven mortality measures as the compilation publishes them, with the years each covers.
// Typed out because the corpus holds them as one measure and the spans differ row by row.
const causes = [
  ['Premature death (years lost before 75)', '2020–2022', '10,482', '9,740', '8,352', '1,908'],
  ['Premature age-adjusted mortality', '2020–2022', '517.6', '470.8', '406.6', '1,908'],
  ['Life expectancy, years', '2020–2022', '74.3', '75.2', '77.1', '—'],
  ['Injury deaths', '2018–2022', '95.1', '100.7', '83.6', '485'],
  ['Drug overdose deaths', '2020–2022', '38.1', '44.7', '30.8', '116'],
  ['Suicides', '2018–2022', '16.8', '14.7', '14.0', '83'],
  ['Firearm fatalities', '2018–2022', '15.5', '14.7', '13.4', '79'],
  ['Motor vehicle crash deaths', '2016–2022', '12.1', '11.1', '12.3', '87'],
  ['Homicides', '2016–2022', '7.8', '7.2', '6.5', '56'],
  ['Infant mortality, per 1,000 births', '2016–2022', '6.4', '7.0', '5.6', '55'],
  ['Child mortality', '2019–2022', '54.8', '59.2', '51.6', '58'],
]

const lifeSpec = bar(
  life.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  life.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Years of life expectancy at birth', labelValues: true },
)

const spreadSpec = bar(
  gap.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  gap.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of adults, as estimated', tickSuffix: '%' },
)
---

<Base
  title="Health"
  lede="How long people live in Allen County, how far that varies inside it, and what a model can and cannot be asked."
>
  <section class="tiles">
    <StatTile value="74.3" label="Life expectancy at birth" note="2020–2022. Ohio 75.2, the United States 77.1." tier={dying.tier} />
    <StatTile value="1,908" label="Deaths before 75, 2020–2022" note="1,306 in 2011–2013, on a population 4 per cent larger." tier={dying.tier} />
    <StatTile value="6.91 yrs" label="Black–white life expectancy gap" note="Wider than Ohio's 5.48 or the nation's 5.08." tier={dying.tier} />
    <StatTile value="17" label="Overdose deaths, year to Dec 2025" note="Fifty-four in the year to February 2023." tier={overdose.tier} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From healthier than America to a quarter worse</h2>
    <p>
      Until this phase the corpus could tell you what share of Lima's adults a model estimates to
      have diabetes and could not tell you how many people died here. The agency that knows will
      answer a machine for the whole United States and refuses it for a county, in a sentence, by
      policy; the county figures reach this page through a compiler, and the compiler was checked
      against that agency at the grain it does serve — exactly, twice.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={dying} open />
    <ChartFigure
      id="ypll"
      title="Years of life lost before 75, per 100,000, age-adjusted"
      caption="Every third three-year window from 1997–1999 to 2020–2022. The windows overlap by two years, so consecutive points are not independent; the publisher flags breaks at 2004–2006 and 2019–2021, both changes to how the population denominator was estimated."
      columns={['Window', 'Allen County', 'United States']}
      rows={dying.figures.map((f, i) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US'), nationalYpll[i].toLocaleString('en-US')])}
      height={320}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="ypll" set:html={JSON.stringify(ypllSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The count says it without a denominator, which matters when the publisher's two breaks in this
      series are both denominator changes. Deaths before seventy-five in Allen County were 1,306 in
      the three years 2011–2013 and 1,908 in 2020–2022 — a rise of 46.1 per cent, while the
      county's under-75 population fell 4.0 per cent.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      title="Eleven ways of dying early, and the years each covers"
      caption="Rates per 100,000 except life expectancy and infant mortality. The year spans differ row by row and no row should be read against another as though they did not. The last column is the count of deaths behind the county's rate."
      columns={['Measure', 'Years', 'Allen', 'Ohio', 'US', 'Allen count']}
      rows={causes}
    />
    <p class="note">
      No other race category is published for this county. Ohio and the nation carry Hispanic, Asian
      and American Indian figures; Allen County carries Black and white, and a Hispanic life
      expectancy of 87.6 years that rests on a population too small for a life table. It is not
      quoted as a finding here.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The overdose epidemic, ending</h2>
    <p>
      One of those eleven rows has a second, faster source behind it. CDC publishes provisional
      county counts monthly, each a total for the twelve months ending that month, and Allen
      County's seventy-two windows are never suppressed.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={overdose} open />
    <ChartFigure
      id="overdose"
      title="Fall in drug overdose deaths from each peak, to December 2025"
      caption="Allen County's peak is the twelve months ending February 2023, Ohio's April 2021, the nation's June 2023 — so the three are measured from different months to the same endpoint. Ohio's figure is taken from CDC's state file rather than by summing its counties: the number of Ohio counties reporting falls from 73 to 45 across this period as counties drop below the suppression threshold, which would invent a decline."
      columns={['', 'Fall from peak']}
      rows={overdose.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value}%`])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="overdose" set:html={JSON.stringify(overdoseSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      What the fall does not do is close the gap above. The county's premature death rate is at its
      highest in 2020–2022, when overdoses were still rising, and this site holds no figure after
      2022 for deaths of any other kind. Whether the county's early deaths fell with its overdoses
      is not established.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Thirteen years, inside one county</h2>
    <p>
      For twelve phases this corpus counted the people of Allen County and held nothing about how
      long any of them lived. The National Center for Health Statistics built a life table for
      almost every census tract in the United States from death certificates and population, and
      Allen County has thirty of them.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={life} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="life"
      title="Life expectancy at birth by census tract, 2010–2015"
      caption="NCHS USALEEP. The two ends are more than five combined standard errors apart, on published errors of about 1.7 years each. 'Lima tracts' are the thirteen whose internal point falls inside the city, which is a statement about where a tract's centre is and not about where its people live."
      columns={['', 'Years']}
      rows={life.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="life" set:html={JSON.stringify(lifeSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The two groups barely overlap. The best Lima tract expects 78.2 years and the worst tract
      outside the city expects 74.0, so eleven of the thirteen inside sit below every tract outside
      but one. Weighted by population it is 73.9 years against 79.1.
    </p>
    <p>
      No county figure is given here, and the reason is worth stating. Two of the file's tracts were
      split when the census redrew its geography in 2020 and seven of the county's current tracts
      have no 2010 predecessor with a published estimate, so the thirty rows cover 82,484 of the
      county's 102,206 people. Four fifths of a county is not a county. Against Ohio's 2,772 tracts,
      which run from 60.0 to 89.2 years, Allen County's median sits half a year above the state's.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the difference is made of, and what it is not</h2>
    <p>
      The county's other health source covers forty measures at three geographies, and it is a
      model rather than a measurement — CDC fits national survey responses to local demographic
      profiles, so nobody in these tracts was asked anything.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={gap} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="spread"
      title="Lowest and highest tract estimate, four measures"
      caption="CDC PLACES, crude prevalence, all thirty-five of the county's 2020 tracts. One tract holds the county's highest estimate on nine of ten measures examined; what is in it, this corpus cannot say."
      columns={['', 'Per cent']}
      rows={gap.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="spread" set:html={JSON.stringify(spreadSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Two measures corroborate each other, which is rare in a file like this. A quarter of Lima
      residents over sixty-five are estimated to have lost all their teeth, against a sixth
      countywide; and half the city's adults saw a dentist in the year against 58 per cent of the
      county's. Both gaps clear their intervals and they point the same way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The question this page will not answer</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious reading is that Lima's health gap is its poverty gap. This site holds the poverty
      gap — the city has a third of the county's people and 64.1 per cent of those below the
      poverty line, on a median household income 48 per cent of the township across its southern
      line — and it will not join the two.
    </p>
    <p>
      CDC names its own inputs: the Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System, Census population
      counts, <em>and the American Community Survey</em>. The survey that supplies this site's
      income and poverty figures is a covariate in the model that produces these health estimates.
      Reading the poverty back out of the health number is reading the model's input out of its
      output, and it would be worthless however true the conclusion happens to be.
    </p>
    <p>
      The life expectancy figures are not subject to that. USALEEP counts deaths — actual
      certificates, in the tract where they happened — and models only the smoothing. That is why
      this page states a life expectancy for a Lima tract and declines to state a diabetes
      prevalence for one. The difference is not confidence; both files publish a standard error. It
      is what was counted.
    </p>
  </section>
  <script>
    import { renderCharts } from '../scripts/charts'
    renderCharts()
  </script>

  <style>
    .tiles {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(210px, 1fr));
      gap: 1rem;
      margin-bottom: 3rem;
    }
    section {
      margin-top: 3rem;
    }
    .note {
      max-width: var(--measure);
      color: var(--ink-secondary);
      background: var(--surface-sunken);
      padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      font-size: 0.9375rem;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
